proofs.proof_tex:
Let \(d=|\mathcal R|=p(p+1)/2\).  By \(\mathrm{ass:fixed\text{-}dimension}\), \(d\) is fixed as \(N\to\infty\).  For all sufficiently large \(N\), \(\alpha_N=(N+1)^{-2}\), and the deterministic quantities in \(\mathrm{def:ray\text{-}region}\) satisfy
\[
 t_N^2={\log N\over4\Lambda},\qquad
 m_N=c(1+t_N)^{-Q}N^{-1/4},\qquad
 \varepsilon_N=2\sqrt{{\log(4d(N+1)^2)\over N}}. \tag{1}
\]
Because \(c>0\), \(Q<\infty\), and \(\Lambda<\infty\) are fixed,
\[
 {\varepsilon_N\over m_N}
 ={2\over c}N^{-1/4}(1+t_N)^Q\sqrt{\log(4d(N+1)^2)}
 =o(1), \tag{2}
\]
since every fixed power of \(\log N\) is \(o(N^{1/4})\).  Thus \(t_N\ge1\) and \(m_N\ge2\varepsilon_N\) eventually, uniformly over the model, so the active branch is eventually selected.  Since \(c<C\),
\[
 w_N=t_N^{-2}\left\{\log(C/c)+{4\varepsilon_N\over m_N}\right\}
 =O((\log N)^{-1}),
 \qquad
 \Delta_N={3p\over2}w_N=O((\log N)^{-1}). \tag{3}
\]
All constants here depend only on the declared fixed class constants.

Let \(E_N\) be the event in \(\mathrm{thm:fourier\text{-}region}\).  On \(E_N\),
\[
 \|\widehat\Gamma_N-\Gamma\|_{\mathrm{op}}\le2\Delta_N,
\]
whereas uniformly in \(P_X\in\mathcal M\),
\[
 P_X^N(E_N^c)\le\alpha_N=(N+1)^{-2}. \tag{4}
\]
On the complement, spectral clipping and the spectral restriction on \(\Gamma\) give the deterministic bound
\[
 \|\widehat\Gamma_N-\Gamma\|_{\mathrm{op}}
 \le\|\widehat\Gamma_N\|_{\mathrm{op}}+\|\Gamma\|_{\mathrm{op}}
 \le2\Lambda. \tag{5}
\]
Splitting the expectation between \(E_N\) and its complement and using (3)--(5) gives
\[
 \sup_{P_X\in\mathcal M}
 \mathbb E_{P_X^N}\|\widehat\Gamma_N-\Gamma\|_{\mathrm{op}}
 \le2\Delta_N+2\Lambda\alpha_N
 =O((\log N)^{-1}). \tag{6}
\]

The deterministic inequality \(\mathrm{lem:fiber\text{-}lipschitz}\) applies on every sample outcome, including exact branch collisions, because both covariance matrices have spectra in \([\lambda,\Lambda]\).  Therefore (6) implies
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 &\sup_{P_X\in\mathcal M}\mathbb E_{P_X^N}
 d_H\!\left(\Theta_{TY}(\widehat\Gamma_N,\preceq),
             \Theta_{TY}(\Gamma,\preceq)\right)\\
 &\qquad\le L_\beta\sup_{P_X\in\mathcal M}
 \mathbb E_{P_X^N}\|\widehat\Gamma_N-\Gamma\|_{\mathrm{op}}
 =O((\log N)^{-1}).
 \end{aligned} \tag{7}
\]

On \(E_N\), \(\mathrm{thm:whole\text{-}fiber\text{-}coverage}\) gives both inclusion of the true fiber in \(C_N\) and the directed-excess bound.  The reverse directed excess is zero, so
\[
 d_H(C_N,\Theta_{TY}(\Gamma,\preceq))
 \le4L_\beta\Delta_N
 \quad\hbox{on }E_N. \tag{8}
\]
On \(E_N^c\), both sets are nonempty compact subsets of \([ -\Lambda/\lambda,\Lambda/\lambda]\).  For the true fiber this follows from \(\mathrm{prop:ideal\text{-}fiber}\) and the spectrally uniform bound \(|\beta_J(\Gamma)|\le\Lambda/\lambda\) proved in \(\mathrm{thm:whole\text{-}fiber\text{-}coverage}\).  For \(C_N\) it follows from the clipping in \(\mathrm{def:fiber\text{-}region}\); it is nonempty because \(\mathcal J\) contains the empty ideal and every interval center lies in the clipping interval.  Hence
\[
 d_H(C_N,\Theta_{TY}(\Gamma,\preceq))\le{2\Lambda\over\lambda}
 \quad\hbox{on }E_N^c. \tag{9}
\]
Equations (3), (4), (8), and (9) yield
\[
 \sup_{P_X\in\mathcal M}\mathbb E_{P_X^N}
 d_H(C_N,\Theta_{TY}(\Gamma,\preceq))
 \le4L_\beta\Delta_N+{2\Lambda\over\lambda}\alpha_N
 =O((\log N)^{-1}). \tag{10}
\]
Equations (6), (7), and (10) are the three asserted uniform expectation bounds.  The finitely many sample sizes preceding the deterministic active branch do not affect these asymptotic assertions.

Apply \(\mathrm{thm:splice\text{-}two\text{-}point\text{-}transfer}\) with \(\mathcal A=\mathcal M_\star\).  Since \(s_N=(600\log N)^{-1}\), that theorem supplies constants \(c_\Gamma,c_\Theta>0\) such that, for all sufficiently large \(N\),
\[
 \inf_{\widetilde\Gamma_N\in\mathfrak E_{\Gamma,N}}
 \sup_{P\in\mathcal M_\star}
 \mathbb E_{P^{\otimes N}}\|\widetilde\Gamma_N-\Gamma(P)\|_{\mathrm{op}}
 \ge {c_\Gamma\over\log N} \tag{1}
\]
and
\[
 \inf_{\widetilde\Theta_N\in\mathfrak E_{\Theta,N}}
 \sup_{P\in\mathcal M_\star}
 \mathbb E_{P^{\otimes N}}
 d_H\!\left(\widetilde\Theta_N,Theta_{TY}(\Gamma(P),\preceq)\right)
 \ge {c_\Theta\over\log N}. \tag{2}
\]
The second bound uses the separation of the two singleton causal targets established by the splice theorem; it does not define effect loss in terms of covariance loss.

On \(\mathcal M_\star\), \(p=3\) and \(c=2/5<60=C\).  Restricting \(\mathrm{thm:expected\text{-}log\text{-}rate}\) from \(\mathcal M\) to \(\mathcal M_\star\) gives constants \(K_\Gamma,K_\Theta<\infty\) such that
\[
 \sup_{P\in\mathcal M_\star}
 \mathbb E_{P^{\otimes N}}\|\widehat\Gamma_N-\Gamma(P)\|_{\mathrm{op}}
 \le {K_\Gamma\over\log N}, \tag{3}
\]
\[
 \sup_{P\in\mathcal M_\star}
 \mathbb E_{P^{\otimes N}}d_H(C_N,\Theta_{TY}(\Gamma(P),\preceq))
 \le {K_\Theta\over\log N}. \tag{4}
\]
The measurability conclusions of the Fourier-region and whole-fiber theorems show that \(\widehat\Gamma_N\in\mathfrak E_{\Gamma,N}\) and \(C_N\in\mathfrak E_{\Theta,N}\).  They are single data-driven procedures, not law-dependent oracles.  Using them as witnesses in the two infima turns (3)--(4) into matching upper bounds.  Together with (1)--(2), this proves both \(\asymp(\log N)^{-1}\) claims.

Now take \((S_N)\in\mathfrak C_\alpha(\mathcal M_\star)\).  The honest-confidence conclusion of \(\mathrm{thm:splice\text{-}two\text{-}point\text{-}transfer}\) gives
\[
 \liminf_{N\to\infty}P_{0,N}^{\otimes N}
 \{\operatorname{diam}(S_N)\ge|b(s_N)-b(0)|\}\ge1-2\alpha. \tag{5}
\]
Direct calculation from \(b(s)=(3/5+s)/(1+s)\) gives
\[
 |b(s_N)-b(0)|={2s_N\over5(1+s_N)},
 \qquad s_N={1\over600\log N}, \tag{6}
\]
so the diameter threshold is \(\asymp(\log N)^{-1}\).  This proves every assertion of the theorem.  The superclass conclusions, including the lower bound over \(\mathcal M_{\mathrm{SR}}\), are stated and proved separately in \(\mathrm{thm:splice\text{-}two\text{-}point\text{-}transfer}\); no upper rate over that larger class is used here.  The upper construction here relies on the uniform Fourier envelopes and compact spectral bounds of \(\mathcal M\), not on a global loading cap.
